{#- The names Caddy on edge serves (formulas/caddy): https://<name>.<edge.domain>
   -> upstream. One wildcard certificate (*.home.<domain>, DNS-01 at
   Porkbun), nothing public — the names resolve only inside the house and
   the tailnet. Import pattern as features.jinja:

     {%- from 'edge-routes.jinja' import edge, routes, urls %}

   Consumers (a new route needs no change in any of them):
     pillar/caddy.sls       the Caddyfile
     pillar/hosts.sls       /etc/hosts on the LAN minions (formulas/hosts)
     pillar/headscale.sls   MagicDNS records for tailnet clients
     pillar/homepage.sls    hrefs (urls.<name>) — and so the blackbox name probes
     pillar/prometheus.sls  blackbox probes of the upstreams themselves

   auth: what guards the route once Authentik runs (step 6 of the zero-trust
   plan) — 'forward' = Caddy forward_auth, 'oidc' = the app logs in against
   Authentik itself, 'none' = the app's own auth. Until then informational.

   seerr: own login (Jellyfin accounts, also for non-admins); v3.5.2 has no
   OIDC, and forward auth would lock out everyone but the admins.

   push: ntfy's API for the phone app, which can't do forward auth — ntfy's
   own token auth; same upstream as ntfy, separate name.

   local: True = the upstream is a container on edge itself (by name on the
   shared Podman network) — not probed from monitoring, only via its name.

   tls_insecure: True = the upstream is HTTPS with its own self-signed
   certificate (Proxmox, TrueNAS, OpenBao): Caddy talks TLS to it without
   verifying. Their APIs (Tofu, bao CLI) keep using the IP, which the
   Headscale policy allows for those ports.

   Not here: FRITZ!Box (DNS-rebind protection rejects foreign Host names)
   and Wazuh (off) — reached directly. #}
